{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1790281742,"nonce":"W9Gj34YBD6tlRZ+w7aHm+oV3MpM=","target":"081f877048ef77c65f25d42ab02587985200cb22db111d4360f7a60955573204","message":"The gpu plugin adopts HXSL, the shader language Haxe programmers know from\nHeaps, as a shading language: a program writes its shaders in HXSL and the\nplugin hands wgpu WGSL. Heaps itself (its engine, drivers and material\nsystem) is not being brought over.\n\nWhat HXSL is, in heaps 2.1.0 and git 9c51d45f:\n\n- A shader is a class with `static var SRC = { ... }`: Haxe expression\n  syntax, with `@param`, `@global`, `@input`, `@var` (varyings), `@const`\n  and `@perInstance` declarations and `vertex()` / `fragment()` (or\n  `main()` for compute) functions.\n- `hxsl.MacroParser` and `hxsl.Checker` turn that into a typed AST\n  (`hxsl.Ast`, `ShaderData`). `Linker`, `Flatten`, `Splitter` and `Dce`\n  combine shaders, pack uniforms and split stages; `GlslOut` (926 lines) and\n  `HlslOut` (1212 lines) print the result. There is no WGSL output in Heaps\n  and none found elsewhere.\n\nTo decide:\n\n- Where HXSL becomes WGSL. In the plugin, in Rust, every language (Haxe,\n  Wren, Zyntax) can hand it HXSL text. In Haxe, on heaps' `hxsl` compiler\n  package with a new `WgslOut`, a Haxe program's shaders are checked at\n  compile time, but only Haxe has HXSL.\n- How much of HXSL: one shader per module, or shader composition as the\n  Linker does it.\n- Bindings: WGSL needs groups and bindings for params, globals, textures\n  and buffers. The translation chooses them and has to report them, so a\n  program binds values by name rather than by guessing the layout.\n- Coverage: HXSL's types (vectors, matrices including Mat3x4, samplers,\n  cube and array textures, Buffer and RWBuffer, arrays), builtins\n  (sampling with lod and gradients, derivatives, discard, atomics), and\n  compute shaders.","files":null}]}